{#
  Migration 0108 — THE CLOSURE IS APPEND-ONLY, GENERATION-DENSE, SEVERITY-MONOTONE, AND LEDGERED.

  ARCHITECTURE.md §5.11 item 6 · §2.2 finding S2 · §16 MI26 · docs/leads/custody.md A10.

  FINDING S2. Every ancestry gate in this system reads the blame closure, and the closure was
  mutable, un-granted, un-ledgered and un-guarded. `UPDATE clause_blame_closure SET max_severity
  = 0` was a shorter path to a laundered gate than any of the attacks the design was defending
  against — one statement, no constraint violated, and every downstream refusal silently correct
  about a fact that had been changed underneath it.

  The fix is four mechanisms and this file is three of them:

    1. append-only          the closure is keyed (clause_uuid, as_of_commit, closure_gen) and a
                            rewrite is a NEW GENERATION, never an UPDATE. `fn_refuse_mutation`
                            (0107, welded by the 0128 family) enforces that half.
    2. dense generations    generation n+1 follows generation n with no gaps and no jumps. A gap
                            would let a rewrite hide between two numbers nobody audits.
    3. severity monotone    a downgrade is legal ONLY with a signed, second-rater severity
                            revision written in the SAME TRANSACTION. Upgrades are free.
    4. ledgered in-txn      every closure write leaves a row in the custody intake, in the same
                            serializable transaction. THE POINT IS NOT THAT THE TRIGGER COMPUTES
                            AN EVIDENTIARY HASH — SQL cannot, by platform law 8 — it is that a
                            closure rewrite is IMPOSSIBLE TO PERFORM INVISIBLY.

  THREE DEVIATIONS FROM ARCHITECTURE.md §5.11, EACH ONE A MEASURED DEFECT IN THE SOURCE TEXT.

  (a) `crdb_internal.cluster_logical_timestamp()` DOES NOT RESOLVE on CockroachDB CCL v26.2.5.
      Measured 2026-08-09 on the local node pinned to the Cloud version: `42883 unknown function`.
      The builtin is spelled `cluster_logical_timestamp()`, unqualified, and that is what is
      emitted here. A migration that created this function with the schema-qualified spelling
      would fail at APPLY time, in the middle of the band, with a code outside the taxonomy.

  (b) `site_code` IS PROJECTED FROM THE SITE TABLE, NOT CAST FROM `site_id`. §5.11 writes
      `NEW.site_id::STRING` into `ledger_intake.site_code`. `ledger_intake` holds
      `fk_site FOREIGN KEY (site_code) REFERENCES site (site_code)` and `site_code` is a lower-case
      operational code, not a UUID, so the cast produces 23503 on every closure write ever
      attempted. Projecting it — and RAISING when the site row is absent — is both the fix and the
      idiom: the ledger's partition key has a source, and a projection with no source is a
      partition key a writer can forge.

  (c) THE PLACEHOLDER BYTES ARE NON-EMPTY AND CORRECTLY SIZED. §5.11 writes `''::BYTES` into both
      `canon_bytes` and `leaf_hash`. `ledger_intake` carries
      `canon_bytes_present CHECK (length(canon_bytes) > 0)` and
      `leaf_hash_is_sha256 CHECK (length(leaf_hash) = 32)`, so the literal from §5.11 is refused
      with 23514 and no closure could ever be written. The placeholders emitted here are a single
      zero byte and thirty-two zero bytes: legal, unmistakably sentinel, and NOT valid JCS — so a
      verifier that reaches one reports a DISCREPANCY rather than silently accepting bytes nobody
      canonicalised. See the OPEN ITEM below.

  OPEN ITEM, STATED RATHER THAN GLOSSED. `ledger_intake` is append-only, and the relay recomputes
  `canon_bytes` and `leaf_hash` CLIENT-side under RFC 8785 before sequencing. An append-only table
  cannot receive a corrected value by UPDATE, so the custody domain owns exactly one decision:
  either `ledger_leaf` carries the relay's recomputed hash and the intake sentinel is understood
  as "unsequenced", or intake gains an amendment row. This function is deliberately agnostic —
  what it guarantees is that the closure write and the intake row commit together or not at all.

  (d) THE SAME-TRANSACTION WINDOW IS EXACT, NOT ONE SECOND WIDE. §5.11 admits a severity
      revision written within `transaction_timestamp() - INTERVAL '1 second'`, which admits a
      revision from a DIFFERENT, just-committed transaction — precisely the laundering path the
      guard exists to close (write a revision, commit, then downgrade). `now()` is the transaction
      timestamp on this platform (measured: `transaction_timestamp() = now()` is true), so a row
      written in THIS transaction carries `at = transaction_timestamp()` exactly and
      `at >= transaction_timestamp()` is the precise in-transaction test.

  WHAT THIS GUARD STILL DOES NOT CHECK, so nobody reads more into a green migration than it
  carries: it counts severity revisions in this transaction; it does not verify that the revision
  concerns an event in THIS clause's blame ancestry. That join is an ancestry walk, it belongs
  outside a hot trigger (§4.1 law 4), and it is the projector's obligation. What the guard makes
  impossible is a SILENT downgrade — every one of them now costs a signed, second-rater,
  ledgered artefact in the same transaction.

  PLATFORM (measured on CockroachDB CCL v26.2.5, 2026-08-09): `(NEW).col` for every read;
  `cluster_logical_timestamp()` unqualified; `decode(repeat('0', 64), 'hex')` is exactly 32 bytes.
#}
